#version 450

// Phase 6, step 2 -- the camera uniform and ray generation.
//
// Step 1 gave the shader random numbers and nothing to spend them on. This
// step spends them: every invocation now builds a CAMERA RAY for its pixel,
// jittered within the pixel and originating somewhere on the lens, which is
// the first object in this project that a path tracer would recognise.
//
// There is still no sphere and no material. Nothing is intersected. The
// window shows each ray's direction as a colour, which is a gradient rather
// than a scene.
//
// WHAT IS PORTED is camera::initialize() and camera::get_ray() from
// include/raytracer/camera/camera.h, split across the two sides of the
// host/device boundary along a line worth noticing. initialize() runs ONCE
// per render and computes six vectors from vfov, lookfrom, lookat, vup,
// defocus_angle and focus_dist. get_ray() runs once per sample and uses only
// those six vectors. So initialize() stays on the host, its six results are
// uploaded, and only get_ray() becomes shader code. Nothing about the fov or
// the basis vectors is recomputed 480,000 times a frame.
//
// THE DIRECTION IS NOT NORMALISED, and that is a faithful port rather than an
// oversight. The CPU returns pixel_sample - ray_origin as it stands, and
// sphere::hit consumes it with the full quadratic, where the a coefficient is
// dot(d, d) rather than 1. Normalising here would still intersect correctly
// but would change every t value by the direction's length, so the shadow
// epsilon and the ray extents would silently mean different distances on the
// two backends. The one place a unit vector is wanted is the display below,
// where it is normalised locally and thrown away.
//
// THE DRAW ORDER IS PART OF THE INTERFACE. get_ray consumes its random
// numbers in a fixed order -- two for the pixel offset, two for the defocus
// disk, then one for the shutter time -- and the CPU's comment says why: that
// order defines the stream a deterministic render reproduces. Three details
// in it are easy to get wrong and silent if you do:
//
//   The DEFOCUS PAIR IS CONDITIONAL. When defocus_angle is zero the CPU takes
//   the ray origin straight from the camera centre and draws nothing, so the
//   shutter draw is the THIRD number rather than the fifth. A port that
//   always drew two would be correct at this camera and desynchronised at
//   any camera without a lens.
//
//   THE SHUTTER DRAW HAPPENS EVEN THOUGH NOTHING READS IT. There is no motion
//   blur on the GPU yet, so the value is stored on the ray and ignored. It is
//   drawn anyway, because the stream POSITION is shared semantics: skipping
//   it would shift every later draw by one and leave the two backends
//   sampling different things while both looked plausible.
//
//   THE DISK IS SAMPLED IN POLAR FORM, not by rejection. sqrt of one draw for
//   the radius, 2*pi times another for the angle -- exactly two draws, where
//   a rejection loop would consume a variable number and desynchronise the
//   stream per pixel. The CPU chose polar for that reason and the note is in
//   defocus_disk_sample; the constraint is much sharper here, since a
//   rejection loop would also make neighbouring invocations diverge.
//
// std140, AND IT IS THE TIGHT SPOT OF THIS STEP. See the host side: the
// uniform block below is laid out by rules that are not the ones a C++ struct
// follows, and the mismatch is silent. The comment on the block says what the
// trap is; the host has static_asserts that make it a compile error instead.

layout(local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8, local_size_z = 1) in;

// Unchanged from Phase 5. Now carries noise rather than a gradient; the
// format qualifier, the writeonly promise and the reasoning behind both are
// the same, and the notes on them are in the Phase 5 history.
layout(binding = 0, rgba8) uniform writeonly image2D outImage;

// A SHADER STORAGE BUFFER, and the first one in this project. The storage
// image above and this are the two ways a compute shader writes bulk data
// out, and they are not interchangeable:
//
//   image2D (binding 0)             buffer (binding 1)
//   texel coordinates               a linear index
//   fixed to a format qualifier     whatever type is declared
//   8 bits per channel here         full 32-bit floats
//   clamps on store (UNORM)         stores the bits given
//   can be sampled by a later pass  cannot be sampled
//
// The last two rows are why this exists. The checkpoint for this step
// compares the shader's floats against the identical arithmetic on the host,
// and a comparison through an 8-bit UNORM image could not do it: the image
// has 256 distinguishable values per channel, it CLAMPS anything outside
// [0,1] instead of reporting it, and its float-to-byte rounding has an
// implementation-chosen tie rule. A buffer of floats has none of those
// properties -- the bits that arrive are the bits the shader computed.
//
// std430, not std140. For a BUFFER block the tighter of the two layouts is
// available, and the difference is real: in std140 an array of float has a
// stride of 16 bytes, in std430 it has a stride of 4. vec4 is laid out
// identically under both, which is the deliberate reason this is a vec4 array
// and not a struct -- there is no layout question to get wrong. The uniform
// block above is where only std140 is available and the padding rules bite.
//
// TWO ENTRIES PER PIXEL as of step 2, where step 1 had one. The probe now
// carries the finished ray rather than the raw draws:
//
//   entry 2n     xyz = ray origin,    w = shutter time
//   entry 2n + 1 xyz = ray direction, w unused
//
// The raw draws left the probe because the ray is strictly stronger evidence:
// it is computed FROM them, so a wrong draw cannot produce a right ray. The
// host reference reproduces all five draws to build its own ray, and runs
// step 1's distribution tests on those -- which the GPU inherits, since the
// rays agreeing is what establishes that both sides drew the same numbers.
layout(binding = 1, std430) writeonly buffer RayProbe {
    vec4 entries[];
} probe;

// THE CAMERA, and the first UNIFORM block since step 6 deleted the last one.
// Read-only to the shader, the same value for every invocation in the
// dispatch, and written once by the host because in this phase the camera
// never moves. Phase 7's camera controls are what will make it per-frame.
//
// std140 IS NOT THE C++ LAYOUT, and nothing diagnoses the difference. The two
// rules that bite here:
//
//   A vec3 has a base alignment of 16 BYTES AND A SIZE OF 12. It is padded to
//   a 16-byte boundary before the next member starts -- but the padding is
//   not part of it.
//
//   A float has a base alignment of 4, so a float placed after a vec3 LANDS
//   IN THAT PADDING. defocusAngle below is at byte offset 92, not 96: it sits
//   in the four bytes left over by defocusDiskV.
//
// That second one is the trap, because the instinct is to give every member
// the same alignas(16) on the host for consistency -- which would move the
// float to 96, and the shader would then read four bytes of padding as the
// defocus angle. Whatever happened to be there would decide whether the lens
// existed. The host's static_asserts assert offset 92 precisely so that the
// consistent-looking version fails to compile.
//
// std430, which the probe buffer below uses, would pack this differently
// again. The tighter layout is not available to a uniform block.
layout(binding = 2, std140) uniform CameraUniform {
    vec3 center;         // offset  0
    vec3 pixel00Loc;     // offset 16
    vec3 pixelDeltaU;    // offset 32
    vec3 pixelDeltaV;    // offset 48
    vec3 defocusDiskU;   // offset 64
    vec3 defocusDiskV;   // offset 80
    float defocusAngle;  // offset 92 -- in defocusDiskV's padding
} camera;

layout(push_constant) uniform PushConstants {
    float time;
} push;

// THE WHOLE GENERATOR STATE: one 32-bit word, against mt19937's 624.
//
// A global in GLSL is per-invocation, not shared -- there is no such thing as
// a variable shared across a dispatch without declaring it so. Every
// invocation has its own copy, seeded from its own coordinates, which is the
// shape the CPU achieves with one RNG object per pixel-sample.
uint rngState;

// ONE STEP OF THE STREAM.
//
// Line 1 is a linear congruential generator: state = state * A + C. On its
// own an LCG is poor -- the low bits have short periods, and consecutive
// outputs fall on a small number of hyperplanes. It is kept because it is one
// multiply and one add and it visits every one of the 2^32 states before
// repeating.
//
// Lines 2 and 3 are the PERMUTING OUTPUT FUNCTION, and they are what make it
// usable. The state is xor-shifted by an amount DERIVED FROM ITS OWN HIGH
// BITS -- a data-dependent rotation, which is the trick the construction is
// named for -- then multiplied by a second odd constant and xor-shifted once
// more. The effect is that the weak low bits of the LCG never reach the
// output, and a one-bit change in the state changes about half the output
// bits.
//
// Unsigned overflow WRAPS and that is relied on, not tolerated: both
// multiplies are modulo 2^32 by design. GLSL defines uint overflow as
// wrapping, as does C++ for unsigned types, so the host reference computes
// the identical value.
//
// What this replaces is worth keeping in view: this function is five integer
// operations and four bytes of state, and it is the entire sampling engine.
uint nextUint() {
    rngState = rngState * 747796405u + 2891336453u;
    uint word = ((rngState >> ((rngState >> 28u) + 4u)) ^ rngState)
                * 277803737u;
    return (word >> 22u) ^ word;
}

// A FLOAT IN [0,1), and the shift is the entire point of the line.
//
// The obvious conversion is float(nextUint()) * (1.0 / 4294967296.0), and it
// is WRONG in a way that no picture could ever show. A float has a 24-bit
// mantissa, so at a magnitude of 2^32 the representable values are 256 apart:
// every uint from 2^32 - 128 upward converts to exactly 2^32, and multiplying
// that by 2^-32 gives exactly 1.0. The function would return 1.0 for about
// one input in 2^25 -- roughly once every 34 million draws, so a render would
// produce it every few seconds and almost no test would ever see it.
//
// That matters because [0,1) is not decoration. A sampler that is handed 1.0
// hits the degenerate end of its mapping: sqrt(1 - r2) in the cosine-weighted
// direction becomes exactly zero, giving a direction in the surface plane, and
// an acos or a 1/(1-r) elsewhere gives infinity or a NaN that propagates
// silently through a pixel's whole path.
//
// Taking the top 24 bits removes the question instead of narrowing it. The
// numerator is below 2^24, so it is EXACTLY representable; the denominator is
// a power of two, so the multiply only adjusts the exponent and rounds
// nothing. The largest result is 16777215/16777216, strictly below 1. There
// is no rounding anywhere in this line, which is also why the host can
// predict every value bit for bit.
//
// std::uniform_real_distribution<double>(0.0, 1.0) on the CPU specifies the
// same half-open interval, so this matches the contract it replaces -- though
// not the values, which no shared contract could give.
float nextFloat() {
    return float(nextUint() >> 8) * (1.0 / 16777216.0);
}

// A TRANSLITERATION of pixel_sample_seed in include/raytracer/core/rng.h.
// Same constants, same operators, same order, same uint32 wraparound. The
// known collision flaw described at the top of the file lives here, and is
// kept deliberately.
// The third parameter is named sampleIndex, not sample. `sample` is a
// RESERVED WORD in GLSL 450 -- it is the interpolation qualifier that forces
// per-sample fragment shading -- so the CPU's parameter name does not survive
// the port. glslc rejects it outright, which is the good case: a reserved
// word that happened to parse would be far worse.
uint pixelSampleSeed(int i, int j, int sampleIndex) {
    uint seed = 0u;
    seed ^= uint(i) * 1973u;
    seed ^= uint(j) * 9277u;
    seed ^= uint(sampleIndex) * 26699u;
    seed += 1u;
    return seed;
}

// From include/raytracer/core/rtweekend.h, narrowed to float. The CPU's
// constant has 20 digits because it is a double; a float holds about 7, so
// the rest are decoration kept only so the two lines read as the same number.
const float PI = 3.1415926535897932385;

// The CPU's ray class, minus everything a class gave it. No constructors, no
// accessors, no at(t) -- a plain aggregate, because that is all a shader can
// have and all this needs.
//
// `time` is DEAD WEIGHT in this step and deliberately carried: see the file
// header on the shutter draw. Motion blur is what reads it, and the field
// being here is what will make adding that a change to sphere::hit rather
// than a change to the sampling order.
struct Ray {
    vec3 origin;
    vec3 direction;
    float time;
};

// camera::get_ray from camera.h, transliterated. The draw order is the
// interface -- see the file header.
Ray getRay(int i, int j) {
    // DRAWS 1 AND 2: the jitter, from camera::sample_square, which returns a
    // point in [-0.5, +0.5]^2. This is what turns one ray per pixel into
    // antialiasing: without it every sample down a pixel would take the
    // identical path and averaging them would average nothing.
    //
    // The CPU's version returns a vec3 with z = 0 and this returns a vec2.
    // That is not a deviation in the arithmetic -- the z component was never
    // used, since it is added to nothing.
    //
    // THE TWO DRAWS ARE SEQUENCED INTO NAMED LOCALS, and writing
    // vec2(nextFloat() - 0.5, nextFloat() - 0.5) instead would be a genuine
    // bug rather than a style choice. GLSL does not define the order in which
    // an operator's or a constructor's operands are evaluated, so which of
    // the two draws lands in .x is up to the compiler. Both values are random
    // and both end up used, so a compiler that evaluated them right to left
    // would TRANSPOSE THE JITTER -- correct-looking antialiasing, silently
    // sampling a different stream from the CPU, and nothing to see.
    //
    // Step 1's four-draw probe had exactly this shape and its bit-exact
    // comparison passed, which means this toolchain happens to evaluate left
    // to right. That is the definition of correct by accident: it is not a
    // guarantee, it is this compiler's current behaviour.
    //
    // The CPU's sample_square has the same construction and the same hazard:
    // C++ leaves the evaluation order of function arguments unspecified too.
    // That one is in include/, which is synced from the CPU repository, so it
    // is flagged rather than fixed here.
    float jitterX = nextFloat();
    float jitterY = nextFloat();
    vec2 offset = vec2(jitterX - 0.5, jitterY - 0.5);

    // The pixel's position on the viewport plane, which sits at focus_dist
    // along -w. float(i) and float(j) rather than an implicit conversion,
    // because GLSL has no implicit int-to-float in this position.
    vec3 pixelSample = camera.pixel00Loc
                     + (float(i) + offset.x) * camera.pixelDeltaU
                     + (float(j) + offset.y) * camera.pixelDeltaV;

    vec3 rayOrigin = camera.center;

    // DRAWS 3 AND 4, CONDITIONALLY. A thin lens: rather than every ray
    // leaving one point, each leaves a random point on a disk of radius
    // focus_dist * tan(defocus_angle / 2). Everything on the viewport plane
    // stays sharp because every ray through a given point on that plane
    // converges there whatever part of the lens it came from; everything
    // nearer or further spreads out. That is the whole of depth of field.
    //
    // The branch is the CPU's, condition for condition. It is also the one
    // place in this function where invocations in a workgroup can diverge --
    // they will not here, since defocusAngle is a uniform and every
    // invocation takes the same side, which is why a uniform branch costs
    // nothing where a per-pixel one would serialise both paths.
    if (camera.defocusAngle > 0.0) {
        // Sequenced for the same reason as the jitter above, though these two
        // were already in separate statements and so already ordered.
        float diskR = nextFloat();
        float diskTheta = nextFloat();
        float r = sqrt(diskR);
        float theta = 2.0 * PI * diskTheta;
        rayOrigin = camera.center
                  + (r * cos(theta)) * camera.defocusDiskU
                  + (r * sin(theta)) * camera.defocusDiskV;
    }

    // DRAW 5, UNCONDITIONALLY, and nothing reads the result. See the file
    // header: the stream position is shared with the CPU backend, so the draw
    // is taken whether or not there is yet anything to spend it on.
    float rayTime = nextFloat();

    // Unnormalised, exactly as the CPU leaves it. See the file header.
    return Ray(rayOrigin, pixelSample - rayOrigin, rayTime);
}

void main() {
    ivec2 coord = ivec2(gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy);
    ivec2 size = imageSize(outImage);

    // Still dead at 800x600, still kept. See the Phase 5 note: the resolution
    // is the first thing that will change, and stray invocations writing
    // outside an image are discarded silently rather than diagnosed.
    //
    // It guards the BUFFER as well now, and there the stakes are different. An
    // out-of-range imageStore is defined to be dropped; an out-of-range write
    // through a storage buffer is NOT -- without robustBufferAccess it is
    // undefined behaviour, and with it the write is dropped but the
    // out-of-bounds index is still a bug nothing reports. The one guard
    // covering both is the cheapest version of this that is correct.
    if (coord.x >= size.x || coord.y >= size.y) {
        return;
    }

    // THE SAMPLE INDEX, from the push constant, mirroring Phase 5's shift
    // exactly: both sides truncate the same float product from the same float,
    // so the host reference derives a bit-identical index and the checkpoint
    // holds at an arbitrary animated time rather than only at t = 0.
    //
    // 60 a second, so the noise is replaced about once per displayed frame --
    // fast enough to look like television static rather than a fixed pattern,
    // which is the one thing about this step that is visible.
    //
    // At time == 0 the index is 0, so the startup self-test runs on sample 0
    // with no special case, exactly as the gradient's shift did.
    //
    // This is NOT yet the renderer's sample index. A real sample loop runs
    // many samples per pixel within one frame and averages them, which is
    // Phase 7's accumulation. Here one sample is drawn and shown raw.
    int sampleIndex = int(push.time * 60.0);

    rngState = pixelSampleSeed(coord.x, coord.y, sampleIndex);

    Ray r = getRay(coord.x, coord.y);

    // THE PROOF, two entries per pixel. Row-major, matching the host's index
    // arithmetic. The shutter time rides in the origin's unused w component
    // rather than in a third entry: it is one float, and giving it 16 bytes
    // of its own to stay tidy would cost 7.68 MB a frame.
    uint entry = 2u * (uint(coord.y) * uint(size.x) + uint(coord.x));
    probe.entries[entry + 0u] = vec4(r.origin, r.time);
    probe.entries[entry + 1u] = vec4(r.direction, 0.0);

    // THE PICTURE: the direction as a colour, which is the conventional way
    // to look at a ray field. normalize first -- the direction is
    // deliberately unnormalised, so without this the image would show the
    // direction's LENGTH as brightness and the perspective divide would read
    // as a vignette. Then map [-1,1] to [0,1], since half of every component
    // is negative and an image cannot show that.
    //
    // What this should look like: a smooth two-axis gradient, because
    // neighbouring pixels have neighbouring directions. No noise, even though
    // the jitter is still there -- the jitter moves a direction by a fraction
    // of a pixel's worth of angle, far below one step of an 8-bit channel.
    // Visible noise here would mean the jitter is being applied in units of
    // something other than a pixel.
    //
    // The axis a component maps to is arbitrary and the mapping is not a
    // checkpoint: the probe buffer is. See verifyCameraRays on the host.
    vec3 unitDirection = normalize(r.direction);
    imageStore(outImage, coord, vec4(unitDirection * 0.5 + 0.5, 1.0));
}
